    "abstract": "<p>This chapter explains how the annihilation of dark matter in the galaxy could produce cosmic rays, which are energetic normal matter particles traveling through space. It highlights processes like supernova explosions and pulsars that are known to produce cosmic rays, and astrophysicists that have been observing cosmic rays for more than 100 years. It also talks about experiments searching for evidence for dark matter in cosmic rays that must separate the cosmic rays, which result from dark matter annihilation from those produced by supernova explosions, pulsars, and other sources. The chapter describes how Weakly Interacting Massive Particles could annihilate in the Milky Way to become Standard Model particles that then traverse the Milky Way. The chapter then deals with the detection of cosmic rays in space and the unexpected observation resulting from the search for dark matter at the center of the galaxy.</p>",
